  <article class="post">
    <div class="pagehead">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/blog/">Blog</a> / <span aria-current="page">PHP2Shell explained</span></p>
        <h1>PHP2Shell, explained plainly</h1>
        <p class="lede"><time datetime="2026-10-23">23 October 2026</time></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p>On 21 October 2026 the PHP project patched two linked flaws, CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619, which security researchers named PHP2Shell. This is a plain-language explainer of the <em>class</em> of problem and where Howdah fits. It describes the attack only by its shape — how many parts and how large — and contains no working exploit.</p>

      <h2>What happens</h2>
      <p>When a browser uploads a form, it sends a <code>multipart/form-data</code> request: a body split into many &ldquo;parts&rdquo;, each with a small header and some content. PHP parses that body and builds <code>$_POST</code> and <code>$_FILES</code> before any of your application code runs.</p>
      <p>As the official advisories describe it, a crafted multipart request drives PHP's parser to exhaust its memory limit while it reads the body. A body well under PHP's default 8&nbsp;MB <code>post_max_size</code> can still hold thousands of tiny, nearly-empty parts, deeply nested field names, or a single part carrying a flood of headers. PHP handles running out of memory with a <code>longjmp</code> out of whatever it was doing, and that can leave the half-built request in a corrupt state — which, according to the advisories, can be turned into remote code execution without the attacker logging in. The official fix ships for PHP 8.2 and newer, and older branches get no official patch. PHP 8.1 and earlier still run a very large share of the web.</p>

      <h2>Why only a PHP extension can stop it</h2>
      <p>The damage happens <em>during PHP's own parsing</em>, before your code runs. That rules out the usual places you might add a check:</p>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li>A firewall written in PHP (for example via <code>auto_prepend_file</code>) runs after the upload has already been parsed into <code>$_POST</code>/<code>$_FILES</code> — too late.</li>
        <li><code>opcache.preload</code> runs once at server start, never per request.</li>
        <li>A network firewall sees bytes on the wire, not how PHP will interpret them, so it cannot cheaply tell a giant legitimate upload from a part-flood.</li>
        <li>A compiled extension runs <em>inside</em> PHP's upload parser, so it can count parts as PHP reads them and stop the request early. That is what Howdah does.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What Howdah does — and what we verified</h2>
      <p>Howdah 1.6 stops the attack during PHP's own parsing, with two protections. <strong>Request-parsing limits</strong> count parts, files, per-part headers and the total nesting of field and file names as PHP reads the raw body, and weigh a parse-memory estimate against the request's live <code>memory_limit</code> — refusing the amplifying shapes before PHP runs out of memory, so the over-limit data is never allocated, and refusing the request before your script runs. These critical limits block by default, even in detect mode. And an <strong>out-of-memory backstop</strong>: if PHP still reaches the memory limit, Howdah exits the worker cleanly before the <code>longjmp</code> can leave corrupt state, turning a potential code execution into a clean exit and a log line.</p>
      <div class="note">
        <h3>What we verified</h3>
        <p>We ran the PHP2Shell exploit against a vulnerable PHP build with Howdah loaded in its default configuration, and it <strong>did not land</strong>.</p>
        <p>We also measured the amplifying shape directly. PHP's own <code>max_input_vars</code> and <code>max_file_uploads</code> already keep the flood small on a default box; on a box where an admin has raised those caps, the same shape used 127 to 173&nbsp;MB against a 128&nbsp;MB limit, and Howdah's default limits refuse it long before that. Howdah's value is strongest on raised-cap servers and on old PHP with no official fix.</p>
      </div>
      <p>This page reflects the official advisories (GHSA-w8hw-w9fx-j79w, GHSA-6wmf-4rv5-p3rp, and php.net). You can see Howdah's verdict on a defanged, shape-only version of this flood in the <a href="/demo/">browser demo</a>, and exactly which PHP versions we have tested on the <a href="/coverage/">coverage page</a>.</p>

      <p>Howdah is open source; the core, the tests and the measurements are on <a href={REPO}>GitHub</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
